    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d262">

                <pb facs="0262" n="252" sameAs="frus:frus1937v05/pg252"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">824.00/806: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in Bolivia</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Muccio</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps">La
                                Paz</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1937-07-15T18:00:00-04:00">July 15, 1937—6
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received 7:25 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>43. Referring to Department’s telegram No. 19, July 15, 1 p.m.,<anchor n="6"
                        xml:id="d262-f1"/> the Legation has not received any communication from the
                    Ministry of <pb facs="0263" n="253" xml:id="pg253"/> Foreign Affairs announcing
                    change in the Government or requesting recognition.</p>
                <p>Associated Press release published this morning quotes the Secretary of State as
                    having stated that he believed that it is not necessary for the United States to
                    consider the question of recognition.</p>
                <p>All members of Junta now in La Paz took oath of office July 14, 3 p.m. La Paz and
                    apparently entire country normal.</p>
                <p>Repeated to Buenos Aires.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Muccio</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
